# Where the quadratic formula comes from
subtitle: "How completing the square, drawn as an actual square, turns x² + 6x = 16 into the quadratic formula for every equation."
voices: {"a":"gemini:Orus | a firm, confident algebra teacher; clear, natural pace","take":"lines"}

## Where the quadratic formula comes from
    polygon sq #2f6b8f points [[60,170],[365,170],[365,475],[60,475]] style.fill #cfe0ea hidden true
    math t_sq tex x^2 size 34 x 212 y 322 hidden true
    polygon strip_r #2f6b8f points [[365,170],[485,170],[485,475],[365,475]] style.fill #e4ecd9 hidden true
    math t_r tex 3x size 34 x 425 y 322 hidden true
    polygon strip_t #2f6b8f points [[60,475],[365,475],[365,595],[60,595]] style.fill #e4ecd9 hidden true
    math t_t tex 3x size 34 x 212 y 535 hidden true
    polygon corner #b5482e points [[365,475],[485,475],[485,595],[365,595]] style.fill #f2c14e hidden true
    math t_c tex "9" size 34 x 425 y 535 hidden true
    math side tex x+3 size 34 colors {"x+3":"#b5482e"} x 272 y 628 hidden true
    group big members ["sq","strip_r","strip_t","corner"] hidden true
    math eq tex x^2+6x=16 size 34 steps ["x^2+6x+9=25","(x+3)^2=25","x+3=\\pm5","x=2\\ \\text{or}\\ x=-8"] step_gap 88 colors.x #2f6b8f x 760 y 600 hidden true
    math gen tex x=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^2-4ac}}{2a} size 34 colors.x #2f6b8f x 760 y 420 hidden true
    text sx "x" #2f6b8f x 35 y 322 font_size 30 hidden true
    text s3 "3" #2f6b8f x 35 y 535 font_size 30 hidden true

    card title "Where the quadratic formula comes from"

Where does the quadratic formula come from? From a picture. Take this equation: x squared plus six x makes sixteen.
    draw eq

With x in two places, you can't simply undo the steps one by one.
    highlight eq part x

So draw it. x squared is a square with side x.
    draw sq t_sq sx

Six x is two strips, each three by x, laid along two of its sides. Together they cover sixteen.
    draw strip_r t_r strip_t t_t s3 parallel true

Now the shape is almost a square — one corner is missing, three by three. Fill it in: that is nine more.
    draw corner t_c

Add nine to both sides, and sixteen becomes twenty-five.
    step "eq"

And the left side is now a whole square, with side x plus three.
    step "eq"

    draw side

A square of area twenty-five has a side of five — x appears only once now.
    highlight big

Five — or minus five, since minus five squared is twenty-five as well.
    step "eq"

So x is two, or minus eight. Both work.
    step "eq"

    pause 0.5

That move is completing the square, and it is literally a square. Al-Khwarizmi drew this picture in Baghdad around 820, in the book whose title gave us the word algebra.
    highlight corner

    erase eq_2 eq_3 eq_4 eq_5

Now do the same with letters instead of numbers — any a, b and c.
    morph eq tex ax^2+bx+c=0 duration 1.5

    erase s3

Divide by a first. Then each strip is b over two a, times x…
    morph t_r tex \tfrac{b}{2a}x duration 1

    morph t_t tex \tfrac{b}{2a}x duration 1

…and the missing corner is b over two a, squared.
    morph t_c tex \left(\tfrac{b}{2a}\right)^2 duration 1

    morph side tex x+\tfrac{b}{2a} duration 1

Out comes the quadratic formula. It is this picture, done once for every quadratic there is.
    draw gen

The plus-or-minus is the five and the minus five.
    highlight gen part \pm

And the number under the root plays the twenty-five: if it is negative, no square has that area, and there is no real solution.
    highlight gen part \sqrt{b^2-4ac}

    quiz "For x² + 2x + 5 = 0, b² − 4ac is 4 − 20 = −16. How many real solutions?"
        right: None: the square would need an area of minus sixteen, and no real number squares to a negative.
        * Two
        * One
        + None

